    "born": "October 8, 1992",
    "hometown": "Pā'ia, Maui, Hawaii",
    "stance": "regular",
    "bio": "Kai Lenny is a Maui-born multi-discipline waterman and one of the most versatile ocean athletes in history. Born October 8, 1992 to windsurfer parents who moved from the mainland to Pā'ia, he began surfing at age 5, windsurfing at 6, and kitesurfing at 9. He received his first sponsorship at age 10 and became a Red Bull athlete at 13. An 8-time SUP Surfing World Champion and pioneer of hydrofoil surfing on the open ocean, Lenny is equally at home charging Pe'ahi (Jaws) on big days, racing the 32-mile Molokai to Oahu channel, or kite surfing across open ocean. He won the 2020 Nazaré Tow Surfing Challenge and has received multiple WSL Big Wave Awards including Ride of the Year and XXL Biggest Wave. In 2019 he became the youngest inductee to both the Surfers' Hall of Fame and the Hawaii Waterman Hall of Fame. He starred in the feature film 'Pe'ahi' and appeared on Red Bull TV's 'Life of Kai' and HBO's '100 Foot Wave.' He founded the Positively Kai Foundation to introduce kids to water sports. A co-founder of Heavy Water Surf with Jamie Mitchell, he helped create Surfline's Twenty Foot Plus big-wave platform.",
